    <item>
      <title>Inside Attention, Part 1: The Mechanism</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/inside-attention-part-1/</link>
      <pubDate>Sat, 22 Aug 2026 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/inside-attention-part-1/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#transformer&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network architecture built around attention mechanisms for processing relationships among elements of a sequence.&#34;&gt;transformer architecture&lt;/a&gt; is composed of many repeating transformer layers, or blocks. Each block contains an attention sublayer followed by a &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#feedforward-network&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network component that applies learned transformations independently to each token representation after the attention operation.&#34;&gt;feedforward sublayer&lt;/a&gt;, wrapped in &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#residual-connection&#34; data-definition=&#34;A shortcut connection that adds a layer&amp;#39;s input to its output, helping information and gradients flow through deep neural networks.&#34;&gt;residual connections&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#layer-normalization&#34; data-definition=&#34;A normalization technique that stabilizes neural network training by rescaling activations within a layer.&#34;&gt;layer normalization&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#positional-encoding&#34; data-definition=&#34;Information added to token representations so a transformer can distinguish token positions and reason about sequence order.&#34;&gt;Positional information&lt;/a&gt; is added to the input so the model knows what order the &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#token&#34; data-definition=&#34;A unit of text processed by a language model, such as a word, part of a word, punctuation mark, or other character sequence.&#34;&gt;tokens&lt;/a&gt; came in. The attention sublayer sets the table for the feedforward sublayer: it does the work of looking at other tokens and deciding what information to absorb from them. Get attention wrong and the rest of the architecture has nothing useful to operate on.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>The Discrete Mathematics Hiding Inside LLMs</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/discrete-math-in-large-language-models/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 31 Mar 2026 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/discrete-math-in-large-language-models/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/discrete-math-in-large-language-models/discrete-math-in-large-language-models.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/discrete-math-in-large-language-models/discrete-math-in-large-language-models.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;It&amp;#39;s true what they say: discrete math is everywhere you look.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;A recent &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.linkedin.com/posts/activity-7435689322504167424-_v46&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  LinkedIn post&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA; from Michael Palmer described how &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#discrete-mathematics&#34; data-definition=&#34;The branch of mathematics concerned with distinct, countable structures such as sets, logic, graphs, integers, and combinatorial objects.&#34;&gt;discrete mathematics&lt;/a&gt; is the foundation for how computers reason about problems. That thread got me thinking about just how many discrete math concepts show up inside systems that seem purely statistical. LLMs are often described in terms of neural networks, &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#gradient-descent&#34; data-definition=&#34;An optimization method that iteratively adjusts model parameters in the direction that reduces a loss function.&#34;&gt;gradient descent&lt;/a&gt;, and probability distributions. If you&amp;rsquo;ve taken discrete mathematics and wondered what it has to do with modern AI, the answer is: more than you&amp;rsquo;d expect.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Underneath the calculus and linear algebra, the same structures you learn in a discrete math course keep appearing: &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#set-theory&#34; data-definition=&#34;The branch of mathematics that studies collections of objects and operations such as union, intersection, complement, and membership.&#34;&gt;sets&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#predicate-logic&#34; data-definition=&#34;A formal system of logic that uses predicates, variables, and quantifiers to express statements about objects and their relationships.&#34;&gt;predicate logic&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#boolean-algebra&#34; data-definition=&#34;A system of logic based on true and false values and operations such as AND, OR, and NOT.&#34;&gt;Boolean operations&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#modular-arithmetic&#34; data-definition=&#34;A system of arithmetic in which numbers wrap around after reaching a specified modulus, producing cyclic patterns.&#34;&gt;modular arithmetic&lt;/a&gt;, formal proof patterns. This post traces those connections.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Attention heads act like soft predicates over tokens. Masks are set operations. Chain-of-thought resembles proof structure.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How Large Language Models (LLMs) Know Things They Were Never Taught</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-know-things-they-were-never-taught/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 09 Feb 2026 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-know-things-they-were-never-taught/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-know-things-they-were-never-taught/how-large-language-models-know-things-they-were-never-taught.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-know-things-they-were-never-taught/how-large-language-models-know-things-they-were-never-taught.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;The model didn&amp;#39;t learn this. It just read it.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When you ask an &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#large-language-model&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network trained on large amounts of text to model language and generate or interpret sequences of tokens.&#34;&gt;LLM&lt;/a&gt; &lt;em&gt;without&lt;/em&gt; web search enabled a question like &amp;ldquo;What happened in the news this morning?&amp;rdquo;, the LLM will respond by telling you that it doesn&amp;rsquo;t have access to current events and suggest you check a more current news source such as Reuters or Google News.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Conversely, ask an LLM &lt;em&gt;with&lt;/em&gt; web search enabled the same question, and you receive a detailed rundown of breaking stories, political controversies, and sports news from the past 24 hours.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Identical question. Same underlying technology. Completely different answers. The difference isn&amp;rsquo;t that one model is smarter or more current than the other. The difference is whether web search was triggered.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But why does that matter? Both models were trained months ago. Their internal knowledge stopped updating the moment training ended. So how does flipping a switch allow one model to suddenly &amp;ldquo;know&amp;rdquo; what happened this morning? The answer reveals a fundamental distinction most users never consider: the difference between what a model &lt;em&gt;learned&lt;/em&gt; and what a model &lt;em&gt;read&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;LLMs don&amp;rsquo;t update their weights (the billions of numerical parameters that encode everything learned during training) when you chat with them. They don&amp;rsquo;t learn from your conversations. But they can access external information and reason over it within their context window. This isn&amp;rsquo;t learning; it&amp;rsquo;s reading. And understanding that difference changes how you think about what these systems can and cannot do.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;A model with web search doesn&amp;rsquo;t know more. It can see more. The knowledge lives in the retrieved text, not in the weights.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Temperature and Top-P: The Creativity Knobs</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/temperature-top-p-creativity-knobs/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 24 Dec 2025 12:00:00 -0800</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/temperature-top-p-creativity-knobs/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/temperature-top-p-creativity-knobs/temperature-top-p-creativity-knobs.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/temperature-top-p-creativity-knobs/temperature-top-p-creativity-knobs.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;Turn it up and things get weird.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Every API call to &lt;a href=&#34;https://openai.com/api/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  ChatGPT&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;, &lt;a href=&#34;https://claude.com/platform/api&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  Claude&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;, or any other &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#large-language-model&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network trained on large amounts of text to model language and generate or interpret sequences of tokens.&#34;&gt;LLM&lt;/a&gt; includes two parameters most people either ignore or tweak randomly: &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#temperature&#34; data-definition=&#34;A sampling parameter that rescales a model&amp;#39;s logits before softmax, controlling how concentrated or spread out the resulting token probabilities are.&#34;&gt;temperature&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#top-p&#34; data-definition=&#34;A sampling parameter that restricts token selection to the smallest set of highest-probability tokens whose cumulative probability reaches a specified threshold.&#34;&gt;top-p&lt;/a&gt;. The defaults work fine for casual use, so why bother understanding them? Because these two numbers fundamentally control how your model thinks.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;The temperature value determines whether the model plays it safe or takes creative risks while the top-p value decides how many options the model even considers. Together, these values shape the personality of every response you receive.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;ve watched developers cargo-cult settings from others without understanding what they do. &amp;ldquo;Set temperature to 0.7 for creative writing&amp;rdquo; becomes tribal knowledge, passed down without explanation. Let&amp;rsquo;s fix that by opening the hood and examining the mathematics that makes these knobs work.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Temperature doesn&amp;rsquo;t make the model smarter or dumber. It changes how much the model trusts its own first instinct.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How Large Language Models (LLMs) Tokenize Text: Why Words Aren&#39;t What You Think</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-tokenize-text/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 11 Nov 2025 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-tokenize-text/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-tokenize-text/how-large-language-models-tokenize-text.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-tokenize-text/how-large-language-models-tokenize-text.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;LLMs read tokens. Not words. A distinction with a technical—and potentially financial—difference.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When you type &amp;ldquo;I love programming&amp;rdquo; into ChatGPT, you might assume the model reads three words. It doesn&amp;rsquo;t. It reads somewhere between three and seven tokens, depending on how the text is split.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When you ask Claude to count the letters in the word &amp;ldquo;strawberry,&amp;rdquo; it often gets it wrong. The reason is simple. Claude never saw the word &amp;ldquo;strawberry&amp;rdquo; as a complete unit. It saw tokens like &lt;code&gt;&amp;quot;str&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;&amp;quot;aw&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;&amp;quot;berry&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; and tried to reason about letters it couldn&amp;rsquo;t directly access.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;And when early GPT-3 users discovered that typing &amp;ldquo;SolidGoldMagikarp&amp;rdquo; caused the model to behave erratically - generating nonsense, refusing requests, or producing bizarre outputs - the culprit wasn&amp;rsquo;t the model&amp;rsquo;s training. It was a &lt;strong&gt;&lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#glitch-token&#34; data-definition=&#34;A token that produces unusual or unreliable model behavior because its learned representation is missing, weak, or otherwise anomalous.&#34;&gt;glitch token&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: a tokenization artifact that never appeared in training data, leaving the model with no learned representation for how to handle it (&lt;a href=&#34;#rumbelow2023&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  Rumbelow &amp;amp; Watkins, 2023&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;To a language model, text isn&amp;rsquo;t a stream of words. It&amp;rsquo;s a sequence of tokens. The way those tokens are created determines what the model can and cannot understand.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How Large Language Models (LLMs) Handle Context Windows: The Memory That Isn&#39;t Memory</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-handle-context-windows/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 10 Nov 2025 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-handle-context-windows/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-handle-context-windows/how-llms-handle-context-windows.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-handle-context-windows/how-llms-handle-context-windows.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;Context windows create the illusion of memory through mathematical attention, not storage.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When you have a long conversation with a &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#large-language-model&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network trained on large amounts of text to model language and generate or interpret sequences of tokens.&#34;&gt;large language model&lt;/a&gt; (LLM) such as &lt;a href=&#34;https://chatgpt.com/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  ChatGPT&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA; or &lt;a href=&#34;https://claude.ai/new&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  Claude&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;, it feels like the model remembers everything you&amp;rsquo;ve discussed. It references earlier points, maintains consistent context, and seems to &amp;ldquo;know&amp;rdquo; what you talked about pages ago.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;But here&amp;rsquo;s the uncomfortable truth: the model doesn&amp;rsquo;t remember anything. It&amp;rsquo;s not storing your conversation in memory the way a database would. Instead, it&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;rereading the entire conversation from the beginning every single time you send a message.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;A &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#context-window&#34; data-definition=&#34;The amount of information a language model can consider at one time during inference.&#34;&gt;context window&lt;/a&gt; isn&amp;rsquo;t memory. It&amp;rsquo;s a performance where the model rereads its lines before every response.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How Large Language Models (LLMs) Learn: Calculus and the Search for Understanding</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-learn/</link>
      <pubDate>Wed, 08 Oct 2025 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-learn/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-learn/how-large-language-models-learn3.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-learn/how-large-language-models-learn3.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;Meaning takes shape as the model learns to descend its own mathematical terrain.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When you interact with a &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#large-language-model&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network trained on large amounts of text to model language and generate or interpret sequences of tokens.&#34;&gt;large language model (LLM)&lt;/a&gt; such as &lt;a href=&#34;https://chatgpt.com/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  ChatGPT&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA; or &lt;a href=&#34;https://claude.ai/new&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  Claude&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;, the model seems to respond instantly relative to the question&amp;rsquo;s degree of difficulty. What&amp;rsquo;s easy to forget is that every word it predicts comes from a long history of learning where billions of gradient steps have slowly sculpted its understanding of language.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Large language models don&amp;rsquo;t memorize text. They &lt;em&gt;optimize&lt;/em&gt; it. Behind that optimization lies calculus. I&amp;rsquo;m not referring to the calculus you did with pencil and paper. I&amp;rsquo;m talking about a sprawling, automated version that computes millions of &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#derivative&#34; data-definition=&#34;A measure of how quickly a function changes with respect to one of its variables, such as how a quantity changes over time.&#34;&gt;derivatives&lt;/a&gt; per second.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;At its heart, every LLM is a feedback system. It starts with random guesses, measures how wrong it was, and then adjusts itself to be &lt;em&gt;slightly less wrong.&lt;/em&gt; The word &amp;ldquo;slightly&amp;rdquo; in this context is the essence of calculus.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Each gradient step represents a measurable reduction in error, guiding the model toward a more stable understanding of language.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How Large Language Models (LLMs) Think: Turning Meaning into Math</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-think/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 07 Oct 2025 06:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-think/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-think/how-large-language-models-read-code.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-think/how-large-language-models-read-code.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;Meaning takes shape in mathematics long before it reaches words.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;When you enter a sentence into a &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#large-language-model&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network trained on large amounts of text to model language and generate or interpret sequences of tokens.&#34;&gt;Large Language Model (LLM)&lt;/a&gt; such as &lt;a href=&#34;https://chatgpt.com/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  ChatGPT&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA; or &lt;a href=&#34;https://claude.ai/new&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener noreferrer&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;  Claude&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;, the model does not process words as language. It represents them as numbers.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Each word, phrase, and code &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#token&#34; data-definition=&#34;A unit of text processed by a language model, such as a word, part of a word, punctuation mark, or other character sequence.&#34;&gt;token&lt;/a&gt; becomes a vector — a list of real-valued coordinates within a &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#high-dimensional-space&#34; data-definition=&#34;A mathematical space containing many independent dimensions, allowing complex objects and relationships to be represented as vectors.&#34;&gt;high-dimensional space&lt;/a&gt;. Relationships between meanings are captured not by grammar or logic but by geometry. The closer two vectors lie, the more similar their semantic roles appear to the model.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;This is the mathematical foundation of large language models: linear algebra. &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#matrix-multiplication&#34; data-definition=&#34;An operation that combines matrices by computing dot products between rows and columns, forming the basis of many neural network computations.&#34;&gt;Matrix multiplication&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#vector-projection&#34; data-definition=&#34;The component of one vector that lies in the direction of another vector.&#34;&gt;vector projection&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#cosine-similarity&#34; data-definition=&#34;A measure of similarity between two vectors based on the cosine of the angle between them.&#34;&gt;cosine similarity&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#normalization&#34; data-definition=&#34;The process of rescaling numerical values or vectors to a standardized range or magnitude.&#34;&gt;normalization&lt;/a&gt; define how the model navigates this vast space of meaning. What feels like understanding is actually the alignment of high-dimensional vectors governed by probability and geometry.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;Linear algebra and geometry do more than support AI; they create its language of meaning.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>How Large Language Models (LLMs) Read Code: Seeing Patterns Instead of Logic</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-read-code/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 06 Oct 2025 09:00:00 -0700</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-read-code/</guid>
      <description>&lt;figure style=&#34;margin: 0 20px 10px 20px; text-align: center;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;a href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-read-code/how-large-language-models-read-code.png&#34; class=&#34;lightbox-trigger&#34; aria-label=&#34;Open full-size image&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;img src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/posts/how-large-language-models-read-code/how-large-language-models-read-code.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/a&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-size: 0.9em; color: #555; margin-top: 5px;&#34;&gt;&#xD;&#xA;        &lt;em&gt;AI reads code as patterns, not instructions.&lt;/em&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xD;&#xA;    &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xD;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Developers are accustomed to thinking about code in terms of &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#syntax-semantics&#34; data-definition=&#34;Syntax defines the valid structure of expressions in a language, while semantics defines what those expressions mean.&#34;&gt;syntax and semantics&lt;/a&gt;, the how and the why. Syntax defines what is legal; semantics defines what it means. A compiler enforces syntax with ruthless precision and interprets semantics through symbol tables and execution logic. But a &lt;a class=&#34;glossary-term&#34; href=&#34;http://localhost:1313/glossary/#large-language-model&#34; data-definition=&#34;A neural network trained on large amounts of text to model language and generate or interpret sequences of tokens.&#34;&gt;Large Language Model (LLM)&lt;/a&gt;, reads code the way a seasoned engineer reads poetry, recognizing rhythm, pattern, and context more than explicit rules.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;When an AI system &amp;lsquo;understands&amp;rsquo; code, it is not executing logic; it is modeling probability.&lt;/em&gt;&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#xA;&lt;hr&gt;</description>
    </item>
  </channel>
